Description
{{Unreferenced section|date=July 2022}}
It is an evergreen or drought-deciduous succulent shrub (which can also lose its leaves during cold spells, or according to the subspecies or cultivar). It can grow to {{convert|0.12|–|5|m|ft|abbr=on}} in height, with pachycaul (disproportionately large) stems and a stout, swollen basal caudex (a rootstock that protrudes from the soil). The leaves are spirally arranged, clustered toward the tips of the shoots, simple entire, leathery in texture, {{convert|5|–|15|cm|in|abbr=on}} long and {{convert|1|–|8|cm|in|abbr=on}} broad. The flowers are tubular, {{convert|2|–|5|cm|in|abbr=on}} long, with the outer portion {{convert|4|–|6|cm|in|abbr=on}} diameter with five petals, resembling those of other related genera such as Plumeria and Nerium. The flowers tend to be red and pink, often with a whitish blush outward of the throat.

Taxonomy
{{Unreferenced section|date=July 2022}}
Some taxonomies consider some other species in the genus to be subspecies of Adenium obesum.
